{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-148082",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let $F$ be the event that a child is female, $C$ be the event that a child has scoliosis, and ${S}_{0},{S}_{1},{S}_{2}$ be the event that a child practice $0,$ $1,$ or $2$ sports, respectively.\n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({S}_{0})=0.25 &  & P(C|{S}_{0})=0.03 \\\\  & P({S}_{1})=0.30 &  & P(C|{S}_{1})=0.025 \\\\  & P({S}_{2})=0.45 &  & P(C|{S}_{2})=0.033\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${S}_{0}⊔{S}_{1}⊔{S}_{2}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({S}_{0})+P({S}_{1})+P({S}_{2})=0.25+0.30+0.45=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $52%$ of children in the entire sample are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.52.\n$$\n\n- Since $2%$ of females have scoliosis, we have\n\n$$\nP(C|F)=0.02.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen child with scoliosis is female, which is represented as $P(F|C).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|C) & =\\frac{P(C|F)P(F)}{P(C)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(C|F)P(F)}{P(C|{S}_{0})P({S}_{0})+P(C|{S}_{1})P({S}_{1})+P(C|{S}_{2})P({S}_{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.02)(0.52)}{(0.03)(0.25)+(0.025)(0.3)+(0.033)(0.45)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.0104}{0.0299} \\\\  & ≈0.348,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
